  "focus": "Learners investigate a real road-safety problem near their school by collecting, organising, summarising and graphing their own data. They move from raw tallies to frequency tables, measures of central tendency, histograms and frequency polygons, and finally to evidence-based recommendations. Each lesson follows Predict, Observe, Explain, DQB and Model Building so that understanding of the anchoring phenomenon grows cumulatively.",
  "phenomenon": "Along a busy road near the school (for example Thika Road in Nairobi, Mombasa Road, or the Kisumu-Kakamega highway), a county road safety officer says: 'The average distance between pedestrian crossing points is only about 400 m, so the road is well served.' Yet learners see pedestrians, including school children, dashing across at undesignated spots, and a local matatu driver says most near-misses happen in just one stretch. Learners tally the crossing points and pedestrians themselves and find that the mean looks reassuring while the raw data show a few very long gaps. The puzzle: how can an average say the road is safe while the data show danger in particular places, and which statistical tools reveal the true story?",
  "supportingPhenomena": "• Two Grade 10 classes in Nakuru both have a mean KCSE-style mock score of 62%, but one class has scores bunched together and the other has a wide spread; which class needs more teacher support?\n• A maize farmer in Trans-Nzoia records bags harvested per acre over 40 plots; the mean is pulled up by a few exceptionally good plots, so the median tells a different story.\n• A mandazi vendor at Kisumu bus park records daily sales for a month; grouping the sales into classes reveals the busiest sales band that the raw list hides.\n• Kenyan marathon runners (such as Eliud Kipchoge's training group in Kaptagat) record weekly distances; a histogram shows the typical training band better than a list of numbers.",
