        "learnerExperience": "Learners meet the phenomenon. Mwalimu Wanjiru reads aloud, as a county notice: 'On the 8,000 m stretch of Thika Road from Roysambu stage towards Ruiru there are 21 designated crossing points (zebra crossings and footbridges). The average distance between crossing points is about 400 m, so the road is well served.' (Ms Halima Abdi, County Road Safety Officer). Learners then picture the school gate, which is at the 4,000 m mark, on a weekday morning at 6:30-7:30 am: matatus, boda bodas, and learners and workers dashing across away from the crossings. Mzee Otieno's remark is read: 'Most of my near-misses happen in just one stretch.' Individually, for 3 minutes, learners write on a Predict Card: (a) Is the road near our school safe for pedestrians? Circle: Safe / Not safe / Safe in some places and not in others. (b) One reason from the officer's claim or from what you have seen. (c) One thing you would need to know to be sure. They then share in pairs (Think-Pair-Share), and the class makes a tally on the board of the three choices.",
        "teacherMoves": "Read the notice slowly, then say: 'Ms Abdi is not careless or dishonest. Let us treat her statement as something we can test.' Ask: 'Picture our gate at 6:45 in the morning. What do you see?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before taking answers. Cold-call 3 learners for what they see, e.g. 'Achieng, what do you see at our gate?', and then read Mzee Otieno's remark. Distribute Predict Cards and give 3 minutes of silent writing. Move around reading cards; select 2 contrasting predictions without naming the writers. Say: 'Turn to your partner and share your prediction and your reason. Your partner should say whether the reason convinces them.' Then take the hand tally on the three choices and record it on the board. Cold-call 4 pairs for the 'what would you need to know' item and write the answers on the board, unedited. Do NOT say whether anyone is right. Say: 'Today we will not decide who is right. We will work out what data could settle it.' Do not introduce any calculation.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain with Think-Pair-Share. Committing to a prediction before any instruction exposes learners' existing ideas, in particular that an average of 400 m means every part of the road is equally served. Writing 'what I would need to know' moves learners from opinion to the need for evidence, which motivates the idea of data.",
